WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.902
Sequence analysis needs two things before LAG or LEAD can make sense.

00:00:04.902 --> 00:00:09.947
First, define whose sequence you are analyzing with PARTITION BY.

00:00:09.947 --> 00:00:11.037
Second, define the event order.

00:00:11.037 --> 00:00:17.407
Then choose LAG for a previous value or LEAD for a next value.

00:00:17.407 --> 00:00:22.297
If the order is ambiguous, the sequence result is ambiguous too.

00:00:22.297 --> 00:00:26.337
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:26.337 --> 00:00:29.557
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:29.557 --> 00:00:35.502
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:35.502 --> 00:00:40.052
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:00:40.052 --> 00:00:46.902
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:00:46.902 --> 00:00:50.772
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project:

00:00:50.772 --> 00:00:55.403
validate the meaning of the result, not only whether the statement executes.
